Computers were too rare in China at the time.

For Japan, the fact that roguelikes emerged in English speaking nations
before Japan comes from assembled bits of cultural history that are
mostly but not exclusively about the number of computers available.
Back in the 1970s coding computer games became quite popular in
English speaking universities.  A dorm friend of mine was the original
author of Empire for example.  But consider the games and toys that
were coming out of Japan at the time.  I recall transformers.  While
transformers are ingenius design it's a very different flavor of
creativity
than computer games.

Programming has become far more popular in Japan than it was back
then.  Now plenty of computer games are coming from there.



